---
/**
 * Footer: the contact teaser, then the city.
 * The contact surface lives in ContactBlock, shared with /contact so
 * the two cannot drift; the footer renders the compact variant and
 * links the page for the rest. Skyline closes the page.
 */
import ContactBlock from "../blocks/ContactBlock.astro";
import Skyline from "./Skyline.astro";
---

{/*
  The footer sits on the page's own ground, not a reversed one.

  It was briefly reversed, and reverting that hands the emphasis back to
  where it belongs. The compose card carries .invert-surface, and
  .invert-surface flips back when nested, so a reversed footer forced the
  card to the page palette and the two swapped roles: the whole footer
  became the loud thing and its one real action became a quiet panel
  inside it. With the footer neutral the card is top-level again and
  reads as the single reversed block on the page.

  The top hairline comes back with it. A rule separates two things that
  share a ground, which is again the case here; while the ground itself
  changed, the rule was a second separator doing a job already done.
*/}
<footer class="relative mt-12 border-t border-line-2">
  <div class="column py-10">
    <div class="flex flex-wrap items-baseline justify-between gap-x-6 gap-y-1">
      <h2 class="band-title">Get in touch</h2>
      <a
        href="/contact"
        class="label transition-colors duration-fast ease-out hover:text-ink"
      >
        Contact page &#8594;
      </a>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-5">
      <ContactBlock />
    </div>

  </div>

  {/*
    The city replaces the outline wordmark that used to close the page.

    Both were doing the same job, a full-bleed flourish at the bottom
    edge, and running both would have been two closing gestures stacked.
    The wordmark was 150px of decorative type repeating a name that is
    already the h1 on every page; the city says nothing and is more fun,
    which for the last thing a visitor sees is the better trade.
  */}
  <Skyline />

</footer>
